  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1103 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nA large upper ridge persists across the southern states this\nevening. A few leftover showers continue along the KY/TN border\nassociated with an old outflow boundary. Otherwise, skies are\nmostly clear across the Mid-South with warm temperatures and\nlight southerly winds. A shortwave moving through the Great Lakes\nwill push a cold front into the Mid-South on Thursday. Before the\nfront arrives, temperatures will heat up once again with mid 90s\nto low 100s across the area. A Heat Advisory remains in effect\nfor Thursday for extreme SW TN, NW MS, and much of eastern AR.\n\nThe cold front will intersect an increasingly unstable airmass as\nit enters northern portions of the Mid-South Thursday afternoon.\nMLCAPE values approaching 2000 J/kg, steep low-level lapse rates,\nand DCAPE values over 1000 J/kg would support a few storms with\ndamaging winds. 0-6 km bulk shear values around 30 kts will help\naid storm organization. A Marginal Risk for severe weather is in\nplace across NE AR, the MO Bootheel, and NW TN for Thursday into\nThursday night. The front will stall across the area on Friday,\nand storms will refire during the afternoon and evening as\nsurface heating cranks up and weak upper troughing remains over\nthe area. A few strong storms could occur given the instability\nthough wind shear is fairly weak, so severe chances are fairly\nlow. Northern areas of the Mid-South, say north of I-40, will not\nbe quite as hot on Friday thanks to the front being stalled\nacross the Mid-South.\n\nThe front will dissipate by Saturday, and the weak upper\ntroughing will start pushing east with the best chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms east of the MS River by Saturday\nafternoon. This system is no drought buster, but areas along the\nKY/MO borders and TN River/AL border have a 40 to 60 percent\nchance of picking up one-half inch through the event. Upper\nridging returns by Sunday and continues into the mid-week with\nhigh temps returning to the mid and upper 90s areawide. Heat\nheadlines may be needed at times, mainly over the Delta southwest\nof Memphis. The upper ridge does not look quite as strong next\nweek, so there is a better chance of isolated diurnal showers and\nstorms, especially by Tuesday and Wednesday.",
